y","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Biography","sourceId":"source-open-library-touching-the-void-joe-simpson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Mountaineering accidents","sourceId":"source-open-library-touching-the-void-joe-simpson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Survival after airplane accidents, shipwrecks, etc.","sourceId":"source-open-library-touching-the-void-joe-simpson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Peru, history","sourceId":"source-open-library-touching-the-void-joe-simpson"}],"publicationYear":1988,"pageCount":196,"summary":"\"A truly astounding account of suffering and fortitude.\" --The Times (London) Joe Simpson and his climbing partner, Simon Yates, had just reached the top of a 21,000-foot peak in the Andes when disaster struck.Simpson plunged off the vertical face of an ice ledge, breaking his leg. In the hours that followed, darkness fell and a blizzard raged as Yates tried to lower his friend to safety. Finally, Yates was forced to cut the rope, moments before he would have been pulled to his own death. The next three days were an impossibly grueling ordeal for both men. Yates, certain that Simpson was dead, returned to base camp consumed with grief and guilt over abandoning him. Miraculously, Simpson had survived the fall but, crippled, starving, and severely frostbitten, was trapped in a deep crevasse.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-touching-the-void-joe-simpson.jpg","displaySummary":"In the hours that followed, darkness fell and a blizzard raged as Yates tried to lower his friend to safety. Finally, Yates was forced to cut the rope, moments before he would have been pulled to his own death. The next three days were an impossibly grueling ordeal for both men. Yates, certain that Simpson was dead, returned to base camp consumed with grief and guilt over abandoning him. Miraculously, Simpson had survived the fall but, crippled, starv